WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:07.207
Over three sittings you build one program: a payment ledger in memory.

2
00:00:07.207 --> 00:00:11.908
Many threads move money at once, and not one cent may be lost or created.

3
00:00:12.133 --> 00:00:13.440
Here it is, finished.

4
00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:17.235
Each request gets a virtual thread that screens the transfer.

5
00:00:17.235 --> 00:00:21.839
Then a bounded pool writes it to a journal and applies it to the accounts.

6
00:00:22.067 --> 00:00:24.074
You build it from the inside out.

7
00:00:24.074 --> 00:00:27.968
Each part comes from an earlier lesson, named under the diagram.

8
00:00:28.200 --> 00:00:30.697
Sitting 1, in Java, builds the core.

9
00:00:30.697 --> 00:00:38.880
Each account has its own lock, and a transfer always locks the smaller id first, so opposite transfers can't deadlock.

10
00:00:39.100 --> 00:00:43.501
The balance check sits inside both locks, with the debit and the credit.

11
00:00:43.733 --> 00:00:47.687
A transfer is an immutable record, so any thread sees it whole.

12
00:00:47.900 --> 00:00:51.000
And a stress test of 8 threads tries hard to break it.

13
00:00:51.733 --> 00:00:59.806
Sitting 2, still in Java, runs the ledger on a pool you can defend: 8 threads and 80 queue slots, from Little's law.

14
00:00:59.806 --> 00:01:02.520
When it is full, the caller gets a 503.

15
00:01:02.733 --> 00:01:05.642
Each task writes the journal before it applies.

16
00:01:05.642 --> 00:01:10.469
On shutdown the pool drains, and hands back every transfer that never started.

17
00:01:10.700 --> 00:01:17.128
A snapshot takes every lock in id order, and publishes an immutable copy through a volatile field.

18
00:01:17.833 --> 00:01:22.134
Sitting 3 adds the front, in Kotlin, over the same Java ledger.

19
00:01:22.134 --> 00:01:26.913
Every request gets a new virtual thread, because requests mostly wait.

20
00:01:27.133 --> 00:01:32.132
The slow fraud service sits behind one shared semaphore of 20 permits.

21
00:01:32.333 --> 00:01:35.719
Each request has one deadline of 300 milliseconds.

22
00:01:35.719 --> 00:01:40.731
When it runs out, the fraud call is interrupted, so its permit comes back.

23
00:01:40.933 --> 00:01:43.231
Fraud and limits run side by side.

24
00:01:43.231 --> 00:01:46.813
The first no wins, and no check outlives its request.

25
00:01:47.533 --> 00:01:50.166
Through every sitting, one rule holds.

26
00:01:50.166 --> 00:01:56.955
Money moves only inside apply, under both account locks, so the sum of all balances never changes.
